.TH VOIX 1 "August 2026" "Voix 4.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
voix \- a Privilege Policy Enforcement Runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B voix
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Voix is a Privilege Policy Enforcement Runtime for Unix-like operating systems. It is architected as a policy-driven execution system that can functionally replace \fBsudo\fR or \fBdoas\fR. Voix uses Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) for authentication and a YAML-based configuration file for defining access rules.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-config \fIFILE\fR
Use the specified file as the configuration source (default: /etc/voix.conf). The file must pass the same root-ownership and permission checks as the default configuration.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-check-config
Validate the configuration file and exit. Also runs semantic policy linting (empty ACL, unconfined root target, redundant rules, open permits, missing blocklist).
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-preserve-env
Request preservation of the user's environment variables. Effective only when the matched rule carries a \fIkeepenv\fR policy grant; the flag alone cannot widen what the policy allows.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-home
Set HOME to the target user's home directory.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-login
Execute the command in a login shell environment.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list
List the commands that the current user is permitted to execute. Mirrors runtime first-match semantics: a permit whose scope an earlier matching deny rule already wins is not listed.
.TP
.B \-n
Non-interactive mode; fail if proof (authentication) is required.
.TP
.B \-s
Execute the user's login shell (ascend to shell).
.TP
.B \-u \fIUSER\fR
Execute command as the specified target user (default: root). Requires an explicit target rule in the ACL for non-root users.
.TP
.B \-k
Invalidate the persisted authentication timestamp for the invoking user (see the \fIpersist\fR rule option). Works standalone without a command.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show the version of this artifact.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show the help message.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
The command ran successfully (or listing/validation succeeded).
.TP
.B 1
Authorization denied, authentication failed, configuration invalid, or a catastrophic command was blocked.
.TP
.B 127
The command could not be resolved or executed.
.PP
Otherwise, the exit status of the executed command is propagated verbatim.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The Voix configuration file, located at \fI/etc/voix.conf\fR, is a YAML file that defines rules for command execution. Rules support per-rule options (\fItrust\fR/\fInopass\fR, \fIkeepenv\fR, \fIpersist\fR, \fInolog\fR), argument wildcards, named security profiles, environment injection via \fIenv\fR lists, and an administrator blocklist of exact paths plus \fBregex:\fR patterns.
.PP
See \fIdocs/CONFIG.md\fR for details on the YAML format. Package upgrades never overwrite a live configuration; the shipped sample is installed as \fI/etc/voix.conf.new\fR instead.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
Voix applies a tiered hardening strategy based on the target user's privilege level.
.PP
.B Privileged targets
(root, package manager): retain full capabilities and unrestricted
syscall access. The full inherited environment is preserved (including loader
variables such as LD_*), inherited file descriptors are not closed, and no
resource limits, capability drops, or seccomp filters are applied. This
ensures legitimate root operations (package manager hooks, process management)
work without restriction.
.PP
.B Non-privileged targets:
all capabilities are dropped, resource limits are enforced fail-closed
(RLIMIT_NOFILE, RLIMIT_NPROC, RLIMIT_CORE),
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
is always set (independently of seccomp), a seccomp syscall
blacklist is applied when enabled in configuration, the environment is fully
scrubbed (clearenv) and rebuilt from a safe whitelist with PATH overridden to
trusted directories, a umask of 022 is forced, and inherited file descriptors
(3+) are closed.
.PP
.B All targets:
signal handlers are reset to SIG_DFL, the umask is forced to 022,
and all identity resolution (UID/GID/supplementary groups/home/shell) happens
in the parent before fork(); the child performs no name-service lookups prior
to execve.
.SH CATASTROPHIC COMMAND DETECTION
Independent of any configuration, Voix blocks unequivocally destructive
operations: \fBrm -rf\fR variants targeting / (including globs, long options,
combined short flags and cwd-relative paths that canonicalize to /),
\fBdd\fR writing to raw block devices (sd/hd/vd/nvme/mmcblk/device-mapper/
disk aliases or the detected root device), the entire \fBmkfs*\fR family,
\fBmkswap\fR, and the basename-matched tools fdisk, sfdisk, cfdisk, parted,
wipe, wipefs and shred. Blocked attempts are always audited in full,
regardless of any nolog rule option.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Voix uses the Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) framework for authentication.
The service name is \fBvoix\fR, and its configuration can be found in \fI/etc/pam.d/voix\fR.
Account validation (\fBpam_acct_mgmt\fR) runs on every invocation \-\- expired or
disabled accounts are denied even under trust/nopass rules.
.PP
Rules carrying the \fIpersist\fR option store an authentication timestamp for
15 minutes under \fI<sanctuary>/timestamp/\fR (strictly permission-checked);
subsequent invocations skip the password prompt while account validation still
runs. Use
.B voix -k
to invalidate immediately.
.PP
Rules carrying the \fInolog\fR option suppress audit records containing the
command text; an outcome-only summary remains in the audit trail.
.SH EXAMPLES
Execute \fBwhoami\fR as root:
.IP
voix whoami
.PP
Execute \fBwhoami\fR as a specific user:
.IP
voix -u user whoami
.PP
List permitted commands:
.IP
voix -l
.PP
Invalidate persisted authentication:
.IP
voix -k
.PP
Start an interactive shell ascension:
.IP
voix -s
